Yes — if you see an **attachment** icon or an option to add a file in this chat, you can upload the Excel spreadsheet here. I can then help review its structure and indicate how to prepare the data for entry into CityCatalyst. I will not confirm or change your inventory without your explicit decision.

Before confirming, it is worth checking three areas:

1. **Year**
   - check that all data concern the same inventory year;
   - separate data from different years, e.g. bills for a period spanning two years;
   - make sure the year in the file matches the year of the selected inventory in CityCatalyst.

2. **Units**
   - verify units for every value: e.g. kWh/MWh of energy, litres of fuel, tonnes of waste, km or passenger-kilometres in transport;
   - check conversion factors, especially MWh vs kWh, kg vs t, and m³ vs litres;
   - do not mix activity values with already calculated CO₂e emissions — they should be clearly marked.

3. **Assignment to sectors**
   - compare every row with its target category in CityCatalyst, e.g. stationary energy, transport, waste, industrial processes, and agriculture/land use — depending on the inventory scope;
   - check that electricity, fuels, and heat are not counted twice;
   - for transport, distinguish data on municipal vehicles, public transport, traffic within the city area, and fuels sold — the correct assignment depends on the methodology;
   - retain information about the source and assumptions with each record.

After uploading, it is best to review the import preview: column mapping, recognized units, year, and sector/subsector for each dataset. If anything is ambiguous, correct it before confirming — do not automatically confirm the entire import.

You have access to one inventory: **Brzeg Nadziei (test), 2024** — “GHGI 2024” (GPC Basic+; AR6 GWP factors).

To upload the report:
1. Open this inventory in CityCatalyst.
2. Go to the **data sources / activity data** section for the sector to which the report relates, or to the document import function, if available in your view.
3. Select **Add source / Upload file** and choose the PDF from your drive.
4. After the document is processed, check the preview of the extracted data before saving them.

When verifying, pay particular attention to:
- **data year** — it should correspond to 2024 or be deliberately assigned as historical data;
- **units** — e.g. tCO₂e, MWh, GJ, litres, tonnes of waste;
- **sector and emission scope** — whether values have gone into, for example, buildings, transport, or waste;
- **control totals** — compare the sector total in CityCatalyst with the summary table in the PDF;
- **methodology and GWP** — a report using factors other than AR6 may yield slightly different CO₂e values.

If you see a screen with the extracted numbers after uploading, paste its content or upload a screenshot — I will help check the year, units, and assignment to sectors.

Yes — it is worth checking this **before saving or confirming** the data.

For each extracted table, verify:

- **data year** — whether it is the emissions year, rather than the report's publication year;
- **row and column headings** — sector, fuel, waste type, emission scope, etc.;
- **numerical values** — compare a few entries with the original PDF;
- **unit** — e.g. tCO₂e, tCO₂, MWh, GJ, m³, litres, or tonnes;
- **number scale** — whether “thousand”, “million”, or decimal commas/points have been correctly recognized;
- **control totals** — whether the sum of the entries matches the row/column total in the report;
- **source page and table** — retain the reference so that you can return to the original.

If a unit has not been extracted, **do not guess**. Check the table legend, footnotes, chapter heading, or report methodology. If it is still missing, mark the data as requiring clarification and do not use them in the final inventory result.

It is good practice to first save data as a draft, review the preview, and only then confirm. If CityCatalyst shows an import preview, correct values, units, and assignment to sectors at this stage.

For manually entering these two values, the basic data you already have are sufficient: year, category, scope, and emissions.

Before saving, it is also worth confirming:

- that the selected inventory concerns **2024**;
- the unit: **tCO₂e**;
- category assignment consistent with the sector layout in CityCatalyst:
  - **Residential buildings** → Scope 1, 1 200 tCO₂e,
  - **Road transport** → Scope 1, 800 tCO₂e;
- whether the values concern the entire city and the full year 2024, rather than only part of the area or period;
- the data source and any calculation method — this will be useful for documentation and subsequent verification;
- that **2 000 tCO₂e** remains solely a control total, without a separate entry.

If CityCatalyst requires additional fields when adding a source, these will most often be the source name, data coverage period, unit, and a short description/method.
